## How a program works

- **`console.log(...)` is how you see anything.** It is captured and shown back to you (the most
  recent lines, up to a cap), and it is the only channel there is. Log the facts you will need next
  turn — a program that logs nothing tells you nothing. Keep it small: a summary, not the contents of
  the files you read.
- **A top-level `return` ends the program**, exactly as it does in any function, and **its value is
  discarded** — gg never sees it. Use `return` to stop early; use `console.log` to say something.
- Every tool function is **synchronous**. There is no `async`, no `await`, no `setTimeout`, no `fetch`
  — calling one throws immediately. A `Promise` you create does not throw, but work you defer with
  `.then()` runs after your program has already ended, so its result is lost and its failures are
  never reported. Write straight-line code.
- Do not `import` or `export`: everything you may call is already in scope, and there is nothing else to
  reach.
{{#if code.readFile}}
- Your TypeScript is **type-stripped, not type-checked**. The signatures below are enforced at run time,
  not by a compiler, so pass optional arguments the way they are written — `readFile(p, { limit: 200 })`,
  not `readFile(p, 0, 200)`.
{{else}}
- Your TypeScript is **type-stripped, not type-checked**. The signatures below are enforced at run time,
  not by a compiler, so pass every argument exactly as its signature writes it — an options object is
  one object, never extra positional arguments.
{{/if}}
{{#if code.editFile}}
- A failing tool **throws** a `ToolError` with `.tool`, `.code` and `.message`. An uncaught throw ends
  the program — but everything it already did still stands, and you are told what threw and on which
  line of your program. Catch it when a failure is expected:
  `try { editFile(p, a, b); } catch (e) { if (e.code === "conflict") { ... } }`
{{else}}
- A failing tool **throws** a `ToolError` with `.tool`, `.code` and `.message`. An uncaught throw ends
  the program — but everything it already did still stands, and you are told what threw and on which
  line of your program. Catch it when a failure is expected:
  `try { ... } catch (e) { if (e.code === "not-found") { ... } }`
{{/if}}
{{#if code.shell}}
- `shell` does **not** throw on a non-zero exit. Check `result.exitCode` — a command that failed still
  returns normally, and gg reports it back to you as `shell → failed: exited N`. Never conclude a task
  succeeded from a `shell` call you did not check.
- There is no clock, no randomness and no network inside the sandbox: `Date.now()` and `new Date()` are
  frozen at a fixed instant, `Math.random()` returns the same sequence every time, and
  `crypto.randomUUID()` throws. If you need the real time, a random value, or the network, get it
  through `shell`.
{{else}}
- There is no clock, no randomness and no network inside the sandbox: `Date.now()` and `new Date()` are
  frozen at a fixed instant, `Math.random()` returns the same sequence every time, and
  `crypto.randomUUID()` throws. This run offers no way out to the real ones, so do not build anything
  that needs them.
{{/if}}
- A program has a fuel budget. Reading is cheap; **writing is expensive** — rewriting more than a few
  dozen large files in one program will exhaust it. Split a big rewrite across several turns.

