## Ending the run

- `{{session.signature}}` — {{session.doc}}

**`finish` is the only thing that ends this run.** Prose does not end it. An empty reply does not end
it. Saying "done", "task complete", or "the file has been created" does not end it — those are replies
that failed to be programs, and gg answers them by telling you so and asking again.
{{/if}}

Call it from inside a program that has just **checked** the work with the tools — read the file back,
run the test, look at the exit code — rather than from your recollection of an earlier turn. The worked
example above ends exactly that way: it does the work, checks what it did, and calls `finish` on the
branch where the check came back clean.

That check belongs in the **same program** as the `finish`. If the program you are writing has already
verified the work, end the {{#if delegated}}session{{else}}run{{/if}} right there — there is nothing to
gain from spending another turn re-reading what this program just read, and nothing to gain from putting
the `finish` off until a turn that has checked nothing.

`finish` does **not** stop your program. It records your ending and returns like any other call; the
statements after it still run, and the {{#if delegated}}session{{else}}run{{/if}} ends when your program
does. So put it last. If that program then throws, the ending is cancelled and you get another turn —
gg does not end a {{#if delegated}}session{{else}}run{{/if}} on a summary whose program never finished
running. Calling `finish` twice is not an error; the last summary is the one that counts.

`return` and `finish` are different, and the difference matters:

- `return` ends the **program**. It tells you nothing — its value is discarded.
- `finish` ends the **{{#if delegated}}session{{else}}run{{/if}}**, and its summary is your last word on
  the task.
{{/if}}

